// @run
// @log 12
// `anonFieldCode` asks `nameNamesFunction` whether an anon-struct field's bare-identifier
// VALUE names a function; a `true` types the field as a closure. Since that question is now
// answered from a NAME SET rather than a fresh arena scan (`emit_base.nameNamesFunction`),
// the set is an IDENTITY TABLE, and the failure mode of any identity table is a COLLISION —
// two distinct names sharing one entry.
//
// `q` and `qq` are the minimal witness: an i32 binding and a function whose names share a
// prefix and differ only in length. A set that conflates them types field `g` as a closure
// and the module fails wasm validation with `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32`.
// Field `h` is the CONTROL — a genuine function value in the same literal, so the case also
// fails if the set is emptied rather than collided.
//
// Verified by sabotage: keying the set on `name[0]` instead of the whole name (which conflates
// `q` with `qq`, and every other pair sharing a first character) is invisible to the fixpoint
// ladder, to the six-channel corpus A/B over 1,713 files, and to the whole 3,608-test suite —
// and it fails THIS case. See `docs/internals/identifier-interning-design.md` R8.
function qq(x: i32) { x + 1 }
const q = 5
const o = { g: q, h: qq }
const hh = o.h
print(o.g + hh(6))
